   <h2>A response to a gap farmers kept facing</h2>
   <p class="about-origin-intro">Kabulig grew from a recurring community challenge: local farmers put in the work to grow food, but often lack a clear bridge to buyers, support services, and new opportunities.</p>
   <div class="about-origin-timeline">
    <article><span class="about-origin-mark"><i class="fa-solid fa-seedling"></i></span><div><small>THE NEED BECOMES CLEAR</small><h3>Learning from the STCBF project</h3><p>While working around the externally funded STCBF project, the team saw a persistent gap between farmers and the market. Farmers needed better ways to showcase their harvests and connect with people who wanted to buy local produce. That challenge became the starting point for imagining a practical digital bridge.</p></div></article>
    <article><span class="about-origin-mark"><i class="fa-solid fa-lightbulb"></i></span><div><small>2023 · SAMAR STATE UNIVERSITY</small><h3>An idea shaped at the hackathon</h3><p>The same concern surfaced again during the 2023 hackathon at Samar State University. The conversation widened: agriculture also needed more young people, and farmers needed better access to job opportunities and skilled support. Kabulig was proposed as a way to connect products, farm work, and people. The idea earned third place in the hackathon.</p></div></article>
    <article><span class="about-origin-mark"><i class="fa-solid fa-people-group"></i></span><div><small>THE WORK CONTINUES</small><h3>Aligned with the ISLA-LEtS CARE Program</h3><p>Today, Kabulig is being developed in response to another aligned gap recognized through the ISLA-LEtS CARE Program. It builds on the earlier idea and brings together three connected pillars: Agri-Fishery, Tourism, and Education. Through these, the portal aims to help local producers reach customers, open more ways for people to take part in agriculture and community tourism, and make learning opportunities easier to discover.</p></div></article>
   </div>
   <p class="about-origin-note"><i class="fa-solid fa-handshake-angle"></i> “Kabulig” means helping one another. The portal carries that spirit by making it easier for communities to find one another, share skills, and turn local work into shared opportunity.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="about-story"><div><div class="eyebrow">WHAT WE BRING TOGETHER</div><h2>One portal. Three connected pillars.</h2><p>Kabulig is designed to help people discover local agricultural and fishery products, book community-led tourism, and learn practical skills. The portal also gives community projects a place to be seen and makes it easier for providers and customers to connect.</p></div><div class="about-values"><article><span><i class="fa-solid fa-seedling"></i></span><h3>Agri-Fishery</h3><p>Shop local harvests, follow crop updates, and connect farm owners with skilled workers.</p></article><article><span><i class="fa-solid fa-compass"></i></span><h3>Tourism</h3><p>Find local experiences, sightseeing, farm stays, and agricultural land listings.</p></article><article><span><i class="fa-solid fa-book-open"></i></span><h3>Education</h3><p>Explore courses and learning opportunities shared by local instructors.</p></article></div></div>
